{"title":"2011 AMC 10B Problems/Problem 23","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2_(Cleaner_Modular_Arithmetic)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2 (Cleaner Modular Arithmetic)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_(naive_solution)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 (naive solution)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6_(modular_bash)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6 (modular bash)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7_(Super_Easy)_(Wrong)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7 (Super Easy) (Wrong)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_9_(Pascals_Triangle)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9 (Pascals Triangle)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:10)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:10)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>What is the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/c/f0c522b9a1aa80756ef8bbe73b690e91daedd0f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011}?$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"71\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/4/fa481fab5ce0083cb2aaa4289d45ee3eaace7dcc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 1 \\qquad \\textbf{(B) } 4 \\qquad \\textbf{(C) }5 \\qquad \\textbf{(D) } 6 \\qquad \\textbf{(E) } 9$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"353\" height=\"17\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/9/5f9607cb9281ad4aa64d97a916131d928ce15c92.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011 \\equiv 11  \\pmod{1000},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"182\" height=\"18\" /> we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/5/d1512d0198fbfef915f6ce26a06d260d60079cfe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\equiv 11^{2011}  \\pmod{1000}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"236\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>To compute this, we use a clever application of the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Binomial_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Binomial theorem\">binomial theorem</a>.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/7/f/87fc9e624924165545fa4b1d6e58ed0f8abd5252.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{aligned} 11^{2011} &amp;= (1+10)^{2011} \\\\ &amp;= 1 + \\dbinom{2011}{1} \\cdot 10 + \\dbinom{2011}{2} \\cdot 10^2 + \\cdots \\end{aligned}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -36px\" width=\"367\" height=\"81\" />\n</p><p>In all of the other terms, the power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> is greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and so is equivalent to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/3/5d357c2ec1bbc5f9f3b5562a24549f1f23a1a340.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"40\" height=\"16\" /> which means we can ignore it. We have:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/1/c015360c171299f3c904c58457bf03151dcb5af2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{aligned}11^{2011} &amp;\\equiv 1 + 2011\\cdot 10 + \\dfrac{2011 \\cdot 2010}{2} \\cdot 100 \\\\ &amp;\\equiv 1+20110 + \\dfrac{11\\cdot 10}{2} \\cdot 100\\\\ &amp;= 1 + 110 + 5500\\\\ &amp;\\equiv 1 + 110 + 500\\\\&amp;=611 \\pmod{1000} \\end{aligned}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -74px\" width=\"323\" height=\"158\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the hundreds digit is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef0f8e6e46b73a9ead0a23a82c2571bb39042961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"59\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>Side note: By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler%27s_Totient_Theorem\" title=\"Euler&#39;s Totient Theorem\">Euler's Totient Theorem</a>, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/5/f158f380ca4864c02498efaef7d33653a040f1d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{\\phi (1000)} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"186\" height=\"20\" /> for any <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> relatively prime with 1000, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/2/5925c91ec6d2a2ff0c3104d22eeba1ba256a6b29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"161\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e65291c6b0edad219ccb356d80d563c4be0e5fd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011} \\equiv 11^{11} \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"199\" height=\"19\" />. We can then proceed using the clever application of the Binomial Theorem, or we can just proceed with solution 6 from here.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_2_.28Cleaner_Modular_Arithmetic.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2_(Cleaner_Modular_Arithmetic)\">Solution 2 (Cleaner Modular Arithmetic)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2 (Cleaner Modular Arithmetic)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We wish to find\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/c/cace02e403300be40f25babf109d6d71bd99e898.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2011^{2011} \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"157\" height=\"15\" />\nWhich is equivalent of saying\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e58bc487ff5215095569fb0e582e05868543a69.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{2011} \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"138\" height=\"15\" />\nIn which we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/2/ed29e04c9784834a4c494638cf6a0492708be36a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{2011} \\mod 1000 \\equiv 11^{\\text{rem}(2011, \\phi(1000))} \\mod 1000 \\equiv 11^{\\text{rem}(2011, 400)} \\mod 1000 \\equiv 11^{11} \\mod 1000.\\]\" width=\"762\" height=\"16\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/0/fe0cedc754aa3c52f02e33ed2511c57455f28e0c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{11} = 11^6 \\times 11^5\\]\" width=\"127\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p>We begin by simplifying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/9/ac91276f8c30f83389f2df05e715bd08ef28ae44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^6 \\mod 1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"117\" height=\"17\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/d/04d66645468b0890f6a82f3be84a8e446d4c35ab.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^6 \\mod 1000 = 11^2 \\times 11^2 \\times 11^2  \\mod 1000 \\equiv 121 \\times 121 \\times 121 \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"573\" height=\"15\" />\nwhich is to say\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/3/693619e585defb62cb9fcb3386ef6d4de3a73a81.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[641 \\times 121 \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"170\" height=\"12\" />\nwhich is equivalent of saying\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/5/ee528955cbf9aa9301f3c110755fd98a926ebd90.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[561 \\mod 1000.\\]\" width=\"125\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>Now, we continue with the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/9/329d81c2731bb10d94022ea29a0a220a18d47d56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" /> term.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/c/29c3fff5e181773a9c27aef3c076df0c6d443720.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^5 \\mod 1000 \\equiv 11^2 \\times 11^2 \\times 11 \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"352\" height=\"15\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/7/d97d7a0cf7fe01d3fae8a985c27d34d0d9aee0f0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\equiv 641 \\times 11 \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"180\" height=\"12\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/d/07d1f4ca97b0a28957727ce229f0c0686b526898.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\equiv 641 \\times (10+1) \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"226\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/3/ab3dd6eefb9e0b4a34aeebf7babb564ecdbcc204.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\equiv 6410 + 641 \\mod 1000\\]\" width=\"199\" height=\"14\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/7923611d1622b48850618fca5ecbdf9378af17cf.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\equiv 51 \\mod 1000.\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"13\" />\nWe then have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/e/80edab0ae5be7c6d975a650b40d3a83224c7abf4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$561 \\times 51 \\mod 1000 \\equiv 611 \\mod 1000 = 611$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"351\" height=\"16\" />.\n</p><p>The hundreds digit is clearly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef0f8e6e46b73a9ead0a23a82c2571bb39042961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"59\" height=\"28\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p><p>Note by future Pinotation:\n</p><p>An easier way is to use Euler's Toitent Theorem, in which one can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/0/7/307694b6b9b60a0a4f6214b6e6e050c63800a4e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"174\" height=\"21\" />, and thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/131a262dee293be8bbd56e769a24c9299ac1013b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2000} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"180\" height=\"21\" />. Thus, we wish to find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/8/258f00b18f8c8715f9294c3fbab6416c56d42678.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11} \\pmod {1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"130\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We need to compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/b/d1b5393c8937f4f8f0000e30418260439dc880a5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\pmod{1000}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"166\" height=\"19\" /> By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a>, it suffices to compute <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/c/67c13dcb160fa193b7f944aeeb1aba1999e7a155.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\pmod{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"19\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/5/945b3febcce042c83e6120ae7d0d4465e1f6b22c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\pmod{125}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"157\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>In modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/a/12a9fb4c5b23667e57f28d844ad99715f18ba3fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$8,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"16\" height=\"17\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/7/1c7938120f4825eab9e1352a38f92899b018236c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^4 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"147\" height=\"19\" /> by Euler's Theorem, and also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/6/24641d61ffec403a7c74c8ed60d02c221d153032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{8},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"146\" height=\"18\" /> so we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/0/0005cb6d3a60d8bfe27bc0ad83d3686a04cb0ff2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2011^{2011} = (2011^4)^{502} \\cdot 2011^3 \\equiv 1^{502} \\cdot 3^3 \\equiv 3 \\pmod{8}.\\]\" width=\"425\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>In modulo <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/3/ef35fa6169ebdc4a156ffc357f72cd8c624be699.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$125,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"31\" height=\"16\" /> we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/4/7/14716c2fcaca03901d810359a72bb1a6c8f4bb40.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{100} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{125}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"179\" height=\"19\" /> by Euler's Theorem, and also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/f/46fcac8b5c6427666c305fb2e5cec0732f55c1f7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011 \\equiv 11 \\pmod{125}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"176\" height=\"20\" /> Therefore, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/3/be33deefa21fd3ee1a5e1a10c9f2fb26f7374e60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\begin{aligned} 2011^{2011} &amp;= (2011^{100})^{20} \\cdot 2011^{11} \\\\ &amp;\\equiv 1^{20} \\cdot 11^{11} \\\\ &amp;= 121^5 \\cdot 11 \\\\ &amp;= (-4)^5 \\cdot 11 = -1024 \\cdot 11 \\\\ &amp;\\equiv -24 \\cdot 11 = -264 \\\\ &amp;\\equiv 111 \\pmod{125}. \\end{aligned}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -71px\" width=\"272\" height=\"152\" />\n</p><p>After finding the solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/f/4/0f454425bc50b295e01c49738cd720a16b34ae33.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\equiv 611 \\pmod{1000},$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"19\" /> we conclude it is the only one by the Chinese Remainder Theorem. Thus, the hundreds digit is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/0/ef0f8e6e46b73a9ead0a23a82c2571bb39042961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"59\" height=\"28\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/c/e0ccc49cc5b5d0d6da4c125df51ec3938c073116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> won't be affected by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/f/05f1ffc8a6829f25b0b0366087a70dfd4e06fef6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />. Essentially we could solve the problem by finding the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ea561b5fb9fd7b2d1f263e735fca58745aa1b4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />. Powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> are special because they can be represented by the Pascal's Triangle. Drawing the triangle, there is a theorem that states the powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> can be found by reading rows of the triangle and adding extra numbers up. [add source] For example, the sixth row of the triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/8/9c8240ebcdef17a04e38dc12fc852bab8180d257.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1, 5, 10, 10, 5,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"100\" height=\"16\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Adding all numbers from right to left, we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/d/44d460a03c4238b1ec9cda9b74179f7ef3b330cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$161051$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"53\" height=\"13\" />, which is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/9/329d81c2731bb10d94022ea29a0a220a18d47d56.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^5$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />. In other words, each number is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/b/02bedd46306390e4c9b9293a00c7de6620b71b9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10^n$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> steps from the right side of the row. The hundreds digit is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. We can do the same for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ea561b5fb9fd7b2d1f263e735fca58745aa1b4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, but we only need to find the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> digits from the right. Observing, every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> number from the right is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/b/72b2cabf550cfbfd5d152976d95b7f4b81ccd8e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3... + n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"119\" height=\"14\" />. So to find the third number from the right on the row of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ea561b5fb9fd7b2d1f263e735fca58745aa1b4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/0/1c090e87561137083ac8bedce48c9e982251de82.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[f(11^n) = 1 + 2 + 3... + (n-1),\\]\" width=\"245\" height=\"18\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/9/c09d3bd7173402523b2b6d0b8e21d0630132fffa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{(2010 \\cdot 2011)}{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"102\" height=\"38\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/3/cb3d29fe8c9ec47286d6235c50357c880fdc2476.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2021055$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />. The last digit is five, but we must remember to add the number on the right of it, which, by observing other rows is obviously <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/e/efe8f67ac18da14c8aa767b8131b0650ac9b9720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />. We must carry the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/e/efe8f67ac18da14c8aa767b8131b0650ac9b9720.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"35\" height=\"13\" />'s tens digit to the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/b/3/cb3d29fe8c9ec47286d6235c50357c880fdc2476.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2021055$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"62\" height=\"13\" />'s unit digit to get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/0/2c024459b191f13b58f34b5e366d4e49e32cad7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />. The one at the very end of the row doesn't affect anything, so we can leave it alone.\n</p><p><br />\n-jackshi2006\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.28naive_solution.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_(naive_solution)\">Solution 5 (naive solution)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 (naive solution)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Since we are only looking at the last 3 digits and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/8/71803232f4a509571f0ed1a26a5148e3d7871ad9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> has the same last 3 digits as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/3/c932acac3f8af148abfd610f23ef8d45a84b8071.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"15\" />, we can find <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ea561b5fb9fd7b2d1f263e735fca58745aa1b4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" /> instead.\nAfter this, we can repeatedly multiply the last 3 digits by 11 and take the last 3 digits of that product. We discover that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/9/0992333611886122603edea21c8f4c6b17fab43d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{51}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />'s last 2 digits are -11, the same as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/9/229f1b3c8dc27fb4c8024ab5cfec6e9a10a64856.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />. \n</p><p>From this information, we can figure out <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be076b135cee8b5bda2115d942c51a22ba4cb4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/3/d3306895b1f7fa75508a7f938da561b09c074d5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{61}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> end in 611. Adding various multiples of 50 to the exponent gives us the fact that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/a/7ea561b5fb9fd7b2d1f263e735fca58745aa1b4f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"15\" />'s last digits are 611. We get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/0/2c024459b191f13b58f34b5e366d4e49e32cad7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n-ThisUsernameIsTaken\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_6_.28modular_bash.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6_(modular_bash)\">Solution 6 (modular bash)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6 (modular bash)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/c/e0ccc49cc5b5d0d6da4c125df51ec3938c073116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" /> is just the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/5/5e58db1710c496eb462f4f272356a4e8b8169a1b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2011} \\equiv 011 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"198\" height=\"21\" />. If we actually take a look at the powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62e34e01db738a98a3d76058e3e3d1126bb40a32.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"120\" height=\"20\" />, we notice a pattern:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/5/2550b35e09ac942d48d06675a5a7bafafcf407ac.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{1} \\equiv 011 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/1/831f3f7a178f72d126168b1ce5cac5df47209ab0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{2} \\equiv 121 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/d/e9dd4c3734fe07125c78b181bfea4fef86780059.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{3} \\equiv 331 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/2/5a2ce3d5a428463a12ab0ca5af3e1e406ca0b879.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{4} \\equiv 641 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/a/1/2a16b83e077499c0cb00ad4d105c4015e8084e87.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{5} \\equiv 051 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/d/eed5931d1b2f448e27b670a2735c1e25dcf57b2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{6} \\equiv 561 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\nNotice how the units digit is always one, the tens digit is always the previous tens digit plus the ones digit (or one) and the hundreds digit is always the previous hundreds digit plus the previous tens digit. Knowing this, we confidently repeat this pattern without actually multiply the previous term by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> out (if you generally multiply a few numbers by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, you can see why this pattern holds).\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/7/7670083305606ee843b8c797c0c92a414e432885.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{7} \\equiv 171 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/0/040c4e1861151bb48c2e92549c0907faa1129d8b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{8} \\equiv 881 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/2/8d2491c4904be2193e26fa27fb4831ae5f8fb4e0.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{9} \\equiv 691 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"183\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/f/69f0a476348d55bec7b6cba09971b98f6b75dcb4.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{10} \\equiv 601 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"20\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/d/8/4d84620c580565c1c9f0f9f93ca17517b021746d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{11} \\equiv 611 \\pmod{1000}\\]\" width=\"189\" height=\"20\" />\nSince <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be076b135cee8b5bda2115d942c51a22ba4cb4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> ends in _<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/8/c6878713578626763c38433b3f4c8c2205ad0c15.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" />, the pattern \"cycles\" every <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> times. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/6/356a6eaddac55ad06132719f22b2552f952a4832.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{2011}{10}=201$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"94\" height=\"38\" /> remainder <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> , meaning that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/b/41bed39d598c1feb945856d78148df69d4743a5c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011} \\equiv 11^{2011} \\equiv 611 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"286\" height=\"21\" />, giving us a hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/0/2c024459b191f13b58f34b5e366d4e49e32cad7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>-mattwang2014\nI think this solution is wrong, since you seem to be saying that it cycles every 10 numbers, although I've tried it out and found it cycles every 50 numbers instead. For example, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/8/268baa89995b8a8b858031e832fae46db86b6efd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{21} \\equiv 211 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"185\" height=\"21\" />, not 611.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_7_.28Super_Easy.29_.28Wrong.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7_(Super_Easy)_(Wrong)\">Solution 7 (Super Easy) (Wrong)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7 (Super Easy) (Wrong)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/d/d3d457177b8756836abac7f9808c256d72c85e83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" /> is just the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be076b135cee8b5bda2115d942c51a22ba4cb4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" /> (mod 1000). If we actually take a look at the powers of 11, we notice a pattern:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/5/965161577087fcc6e8795eccaa513dae605a4bfc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{1} = 11\\]\" width=\"65\" height=\"15\" /> \n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e0497310ed804e4f49bdd1a269b76ccb3226e7a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{2} = 121\\]\" width=\"74\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/e/8aefacc75bd34ff7d3a571f0e1464bf66bbce96a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{3} = 1331\\]\" width=\"83\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/d/5/9d5b9ff4a80e45c88d4a00eed5360a72a5e3921a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[11^{4} = 14641\\]\" width=\"92\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><br />\nWe notice that the hundreds digit just increases by 1 starting from 1(the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/a/62a9ac4b7302eb53807c4c580df5625400f2e52b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"23\" height=\"15\" />). All we have to do now is add all the numbers from 1 to 11 (since the hundreds digit increases by 1 with every power of 11 that comes after) to get the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/0/be076b135cee8b5bda2115d942c51a22ba4cb4fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"30\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/9/9/69925966dec54a8a86fcaec302cd3423dd64970c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3 + 4.... + 11 = 66$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"208\" height=\"15\" />. Thus the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/0/2c024459b191f13b58f34b5e366d4e49e32cad7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(D) } 6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -10px\" width=\"56\" height=\"28\" />.\n</p><p>-TheOfficalPro\n</p><p>Ilovebender I believe this solution is wrong, as I can see that the hundreds digit doesn't increase by 1, it goes from 0 to 1 to 3 to 6. A more accurate solution would be the hundreds digit are triangular numbers.\n</p><p>-Extremelysupercooldude: In your solution, you find the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/d/d3d457177b8756836abac7f9808c256d72c85e83.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{11}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"52\" height=\"17\" />, but the problem asks for the hundreds digit of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/c/e0ccc49cc5b5d0d6da4c125df51ec3938c073116.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2011}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"61\" height=\"15\" />, but you never cite way the two hundreds digits are the same. \n</p><p>~Biegetomcat72: The \"hundreds digit\" would be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/8/978fc8b156e87402c10ef2d7fc56d19f512d40fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3 + 4 ... + 9 + 0 + 1 = 56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"256\" height=\"15\" />\nLogic: Since multiplying a number by eleven is the same as the sum of itself and and itself with an appended 0, the 10s digit goes up by 1 for every power of 11 up until 9 then goes to 0. Then, from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d32d19d0d95070f787f93d275fb677a1a62a8e2f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" />, the hundreds digit goes up by how much the value of the tens digit of the number before being multiplied by 11, which gets us the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/8/978fc8b156e87402c10ef2d7fc56d19f512d40fd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 + 2 + 3 + 4 ... + 9 + 0 + 1 = 56$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"256\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8\">Solution 8</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>First, see that 2011 is a prime number. Therefore 2011 and 1000 are coprime.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/7/d47eec9126b2a0f5fcd8280ea1512577c5d88d95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd{(2011, 1000)} = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" />\n</p><p><br />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/1/f/21fe313dea7ac04d6a7bdbc2bdbd48ab874bebf5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000 }$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"192\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/8/4f8b539a8f18fd3b382bc7d60b83a937fc2569be.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{2000} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"198\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/0/460a574572c87d4a5ad862dd355ac4b0ff10dc9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2011^{11} \\equiv 11^{11} \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"208\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/4/ba430ff5e09c7d514a4b3d566609255e784e6305.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$661 \\equiv 2011^{11} \\pmod {1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"203\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>Therefore, the answer is 6.\n</p><p>~euriley\n~unfinished latex by megacleverstarfish15.\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_9_.28Pascals_Triangle.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9_(Pascals_Triangle)\">Solution 9 (Pascals Triangle)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9 (Pascals Triangle)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By Euler's Totient Theorem and basic modular arithmetic, we get\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/d/ced5d54599e33ea5d1c6c1a409910d979fe219e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^{11} \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"134\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>If you know the pascals triangle pattern:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/3/893d6c6ad95a24c5597ffc5984360fe9a0c40a1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^5  = 161051$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"106\" height=\"17\" /> (This comes from 1 5 10 10 5 1 where you then carry over the one's twice to get 161051. For <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/5/715bddfcb5e45956dd7209f04c16090b39b85d65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$11^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"27\" height=\"17\" /> we have 1 4 6 4 1 only)\n</p><p>Now this is easier.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/7/2c7d813f9e865f1e9a36aea8679b20a6fcf4f75d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$51^2 * 11 \\equiv 601 * 11 \\equiv 611 \\pmod{1000}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"301\" height=\"21\" />\n</p><p>This means that the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/b/adb760a2c7143780db481b394ada34e0bb23b3bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{6}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"22\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>~Aarav22\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E1:10.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:10)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:10)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:10)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/EX8RK8brNYY\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/EX8RK8brNYY</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p><p>Note: the problem where this is linked is covered 1:25-2:35\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_TheBeautyofMath\">Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=12\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by TheBeautyofMath\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/7rosJnqLhs8?t=323\">https://youtu.be/7rosJnqLhs8?t=323</a>\n</p><p>~IceMatrix\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_OmegaLearn\">Video Solution by OmegaLearn</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23&amp;action=edit&amp;section=13\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by OmegaLearn\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/-H4n-QplQew?t=1423\">https://youtu.be/-H4n-QplQew?t=1423</a>\n</p><p>~ pi_is_3.14\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2011_AMC_10B_Problems/Problem_23